    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. Could you expand a bit more on the fee structures? Specifically, I want to make sure I explain to the client why mutual funds might have higher expense ratios and if there are any hidden costs or \"leaks\" in how they are managed compared to ETFs.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mutual funds often carry higher expense ratios due to the costs associated with actively managed teams and research, which can be passed on to investors. Some mutual funds might also involve sales loads, which are upfront or deferred commissions paid to brokers, representing a direct hidden cost. ETFs, especially those tracking indices, typically have very low expense ratios because their management is automated, leading to fewer ongoing operational fees compared to actively managed mutual funds.",
      "source": "target",
